# Single 16 GB VRAM GPU — vLLM (RTX 5060 Ti 16GB / RTX 4080 / similar, Ampere+/Blackwell)
#
# Prepping the ground for a new box: RTX 5060 Ti #1 now, RTX 5060 Ti #2 arriving later
# (see models/2x16gb.vllm for the tp=2 config once that card is installed).
# NOT YET RUN. Step-by-step: test-plan-5060ti.md, Part B.
#
# Format: <short-name>  [<gguf-file>]  [<params>]  [hf:<repo>]
#   GGUF mode (gguf-file set, not "-"): the harness serves the local file with
#     --tokenizer <hf_repo> --quantization gguf (see lib/vllm_client.py). hf: must be
#     the ORIGINAL model repo, which holds the tokenizer; GGUF-only repos such as
#     bartowski's have no tokenizer files. So don't run fetch-hf.sh on this file:
#     download each GGUF through its models/*.txt entry (noted per entry below).
#   HF-format mode (gguf-file omitted or "-"): vLLM downloads straight from the
#     HF Hub on the first `vllm serve` call (GPTQ/AWQ/NVFP4/safetensors).
#
# Setup (details in test-plan-5060ti.md):
#   1. Confirm the GPU: nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,compute_cap,memory.total --format=csv
#      (RTX 5060 Ti reports compute_cap 12.0.)
#   2. Install a CURRENT vLLM (0.29.0 as of 2026-09-15) into a fresh venv on WSL ext4.
#      The repo's .venv has vLLM 0.21.0 (May 2026), which predates the June 2026 move of
#      GGUF support into vllm-gguf-plugin.
#   3. Install vllm-gguf-plugin into that same venv (uv pip install -e . --no-build-isolation).
#      Checkout d4c1f0d already has the Blackwell bf16 fix and the 27B load-OOM fix.
#   4. Download the GGUF: ./fetch-hf.sh models/16gb.txt --models qwen2.5-coder:14b
#   5. Run: VLLM_BIN=~/vllm-env/bin/vllm ./run.sh --backend vllm --model-file models/16gb.vllm \
#             --models qwen2.5-coder:14b-5060ti
#      Without VLLM_BIN, run.sh picks up the old vLLM in the repo's .venv.

# ── Smoke test: confirm the vLLM + GGUF plugin stack actually works on this box ──
# Dense Qwen2.5 architecture — listed in the plugin's own "tested model coverage" table
# (at Q6_K; this is Q4_K_M), so this is the safest first thing to try before anything
# exotic: a model that won't load is worth nothing, regardless of benchmark score.
# ~9 GB Q4_K_M weights leave generous headroom on 16 GB for KV cache and vLLM's own
# overhead. GGUF comes from bartowski via models/16gb.txt; hf: below is the tokenizer repo.
qwen2.5-coder:14b-5060ti  Qwen2.5-Coder-14B-Instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf  tp=1,dtype=auto,max_model_len=32768,enable_prefix_caching  hf:Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-14B-Instruct

# ── Qwen3.8-27B does NOT fit a single 16 GB card ─────────────────────────────
# Same dense Gated-DeltaNet-hybrid base as this repo's extensively-tested
# qwen3.8:27b (llama-server, 24gb.txt/2x24gb.txt/3x24gb.txt). Every NVFP4/quantized
# build found (2026-09-14 web check) has weights alone in the ~17-23 GB range —
# already bigger than this card's 16 GB before any KV cache or vLLM overhead.
# vLLM's own recipe page (recipes.vllm.ai/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B) only documents a
# single-GPU config on an RTX 5090 (31.4 GB usable), and even that needs
# --enforce-eager to avoid OOM during CUDA graph capture.
# → See models/2x16gb.vllm for the tp=2 (32 GB) config once the second RTX 5060 Ti
#   is installed. On one 16 GB card, qwen3.8-27b-gsqrco (llama-server, models/16gb.txt)
#   is the way to run this model family.
